    "description": "Not an Epic product, but the Sprout Living powder many buyers compare it with. Simple Protein Pumpkin Seed has one ingredient, organic pumpkin seed protein. Per the label, two scoops (32.5 g) give 20 g of protein, about 150 calories and 5 g of fat, with useful iron, magnesium and zinc. The 1 lb bag lists at $32.95 on the brand site; at about 14 servings that is roughly $2.94 per 25 g.",
    "pros": [
      "One ingredient, nothing else",
      "Good source of magnesium and iron (label)",
      "USDA Organic and Certified Vegan"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Only one protein source, so a less balanced amino acid profile",
      "More fat per gram of protein than pea isolates",
      "No published heavy-metal results"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Choose it over Epic if you want a single-source powder for an elimination diet or baking.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein / serving": "20 g (label)",
      "Calories": "About 150",
      "Ingredients": "1 (pumpkin seed protein)",
      "Cost per 25 g": "About $2.94 (1 lb list price)"
    },
    "amazonUrl": "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B01N7CLYX7"
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Sprout Living Protein Powder Review",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/sprout-living-protein-powder-review/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Sprout Living third-party tested?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Sprout Living says yes. Every Epic Protein bag states it is third-party tested, every ingredient and batch, and the brand says it spot-tests for arsenic, lead, cadmium and mercury. It does not publish certificates of analysis, and we found no Sprout Living product on the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport databases. Tested athletes should choose a certified product instead."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Sprout Living Epic Protein healthy and clean?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most healthy adults, yes. Epic is USDA Organic and Certified Vegan, with no gums, natural flavors or sugar alcohols. Original has six ingredients, 26 g of protein and no added sugar per the label. Flavored bags add 4 g of coconut sugar and stevia leaf. Watch the 310 to 380 mg of sodium per serving if you limit salt."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Sprout Living protein contain heavy metals?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Like all plant proteins, it will contain trace heavy metals from soil. Sprout Living says its products consistently test within limits but does not publish the numbers, so the amount per serving is unknown. Choosing Original or Vanilla Lucuma over Chocolate Maca avoids cacao, which tends to raise cadmium and lead readings across the category."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Sprout Living contain stevia or sugar?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Original and the unflavored Simple Protein bags contain no sweetener at all. Vanilla Lucuma and Chocolate Maca contain organic coconut sugar, adding 4 g of sugar per serving, plus organic stevia leaf. Real Sport has no added sugar and is sweetened with stevia leaf and banana. No Epic flavor uses erythritol or other sugar alcohols."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which Sprout Living flavor is best?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "In buyer reviews, Chocolate Maca and Vanilla Lucuma are the most praised flavors, with several buyers saying Chocolate Maca is less gritty than other plant proteins. Original is the best value and the most versatile in cooking, but buyers describe it as plain and earthy in water. We have not tasted them; this is buyer feedback only."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What are common complaints about Sprout Living?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The most common complaints in buyer reviews are price and taste: some find Original too earthy on its own, and some feel 20 g of protein in a 38 g flavored scoop is poor value. One reviewer described a chalky, earthy texture. Overall ratings on the brand site and at retailers are high, around 4.8 to 4.9 out of 5."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Sprout Living worth the money?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Epic Original is, if you value an organic, six-ingredient label; it costs about $2.80 per 25 g of protein, or about $2.38 with subscribe and save. The flavored bags at $3.64 per 25 g are harder to justify unless you specifically want a gum-free, natural-flavor-free superfood blend. Orgain and Sunwarrior cost less per gram."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "sprout living protein powder",
      "sprout living epic protein review",
      "is sprout living third party tested",
      "sprout living epic protein",
      "sprout living heavy metals",
      "sprout living protein reviews"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Plant Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-plant-based-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Sprout Living Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      For anyone looking at Sprout Living's organic Epic Protein: what is in each flavor, what the brand says about testing and heavy metals, and what a scoop costs per 25 g of protein.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Which Bag to Buy</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in Epic Protein</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Is It Third-Party Tested?</a>
      <a href="#heavy-metals">Heavy Metals and Lead</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#healthy">Is It Healthy?</a>
      <a href="#buyer-reviews">Buyer Reviews</a>
      <a href="#vs-rivals">Sprout Living vs Rivals</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Sprout Living Epic Protein is one of the cleanest plant protein labels you can buy: organic, no gums, no natural flavors, and stevia plus a little coconut sugar only in the flavored bags. The weak points are the testing evidence and the price. The brand says every ingredient and batch is third-party tested, but it publishes no lab results, and the flavored bags cost about $3.64 per 25 g of protein.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Is Sprout Living Protein Powder Good? The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sprout Living Epic Protein is a clean, well-made organic plant protein that costs more than most, and Original is the bag to buy. Original gives 26 g of protein in a 38 g serving with six organic ingredients and no sweetener, at about $2.80 per 25 g. The flavored bags drop to 20 to 22 g of protein and cost $3.64 to $3.78 per 25 g. Testing is claimed rather than shown.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you want a certified organic, vegan protein with no gums, no natural flavors and no sugar alcohols, and you are happy to pay for it. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you need a sport-certified powder, want published heavy-metal results, or are buying mainly on protein per dollar, because cheaper organic pea blends give more protein per scoop.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Epic Protein by the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20-26 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per 38 g serving</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0-4 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Added sugar</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$2.80-$3.78</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g protein</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">USDA</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Organic certified</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Which Sprout Living Protein Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            These are four Epic Protein flavors and one Simple Protein from Sprout Living's current range, chosen to cover the main choices: unflavored or flavored, everyday or sport, blend or single source. Nutrition figures are read from the nutrition panels on sproutliving.com, and prices are the brand-site list prices for a 1 lb bag on 21 November 2026, before the 15 percent subscribe-and-save discount. Amazon prices vary; the cards link to Amazon where we could confirm a listing and to a search otherwise.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is in Sprout Living Epic Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every Epic Protein starts from the same organic base of yellow pea, pumpkin seed, sunflower seed, cranberry seed and chia seed protein, with organic Jerusalem artichoke inulin for fiber. The flavored bags then add a superfood blend and a small amount of sweetener. The table below is read from the nutrition panels on sproutliving.com at the time of writing; all servings are two rounded scoops, or 38 g.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Flavor</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sugar (added)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sodium</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sweetener</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Original</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">26 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">140</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 g (0 g)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">310 mg</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vanilla Lucuma</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">150</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7 g (4 g)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">350 mg</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Coconut sugar, stevia leaf</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chocolate Maca</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">150</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6 g (4 g)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">340 mg</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Coconut sugar, stevia leaf</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Real Sport</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">22 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">150</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 g (0 g)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">380 mg</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stevia leaf, banana</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Original is the best label in the range. With 26 g of protein in 38 g of powder, about 68 percent of each scoop is protein, and the rest is mostly fiber, a little fat from the seeds, and minerals. There is no sweetener, salt or flavoring added; the 310 mg of sodium comes from the plant proteins themselves, which is typical of pea protein. Iron is 5 mg per serving, 27 percent of the daily value.
          </p>

          <p>
            The flavored bags trade protein for food. Vanilla Lucuma, for example, adds organic coconut sugar, lucuma, Ceylon cinnamon, acerola, baobab, vanilla bean, banana, Himalayan salt and stevia leaf. That is why protein drops to 20 g in the same 38 g scoop. The upside is that the flavor comes from real ingredients rather than natural flavors, which Sprout Living rules out entirely, along with gums and sugar alcohols such as erythritol.
          </p>

          <p>
            The range also includes Complete Coffee and Green Kingdom at 20 g per serving, and Pro Collagen and Mindful Matcha at 15 g in smaller 336 g bags, per the brand's product descriptions. Pro Collagen contains no collagen; the brand says it supports natural collagen production. We left those four out of the cards because their protein per dollar is weaker and the claims rest more on the botanicals than the protein.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is Sprout Living Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sprout Living says its protein is third-party tested, but it does not publish the results and holds no sport certification. The back of every Epic bag says "3rd-party tested, every ingredient and batch", and the product pages say ingredients and finished goods are tested. The brand's help center says it spot-tests for the four main heavy metals, arsenic, lead, cadmium and mercury, and that its products consistently test within limits.
          </p>

          <p>
            What is missing is anything you can check. The help center does not name the lab for heavy metals or link to certificates of analysis, though it does invite customers to email for more detail. We found no Sprout Living product on the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport databases, and no Clean Label Project certification for Epic Protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            The certifications Sprout Living does hold are about how ingredients are grown and handled, not about contaminants. The bag carries the USDA Organic seal, certified by Oregon Tilth, plus Certified Vegan from Vegan Action, Kosher Check and Whole30 Approved, and it is made in a BRC-certified facility in the USA. Those are real and checkable, but organic certification does not limit heavy metals, which come from soil whether a farm is organic or not.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE PRACTICAL TAKE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Credible brand, unverifiable testing claim.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">For everyday use, a small brand that says it batch-tests and invites questions is a reasonable bet. For drug-tested athletes it is not enough; only a product on the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport list protects you.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you want a certified organic plant protein, the main alternative is Garden of Life Sport, which is NSF Certified for Sport; our <a href="/garden-of-life-protein-powder-review/">Garden of Life review</a> explains which of its badges means sport testing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="heavy-metals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Does Sprout Living Protein Contain Heavy Metals or Lead?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sprout Living says its products test within limits for heavy metals, but it does not publish the numbers, so we cannot say how much lead or cadmium a serving contains. All plant proteins carry some trace metals, because pea, seed and rice crops absorb them from soil. That is true of every brand, organic or not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two things lower the risk within the Epic range. First, Original has no cacao; chocolate flavors tend to test higher across the category because cacao itself accumulates cadmium and lead. Second, a smaller serving means less of everything, so 38 g of Original delivering 26 g of protein is a better ratio than a 38 g flavored scoop delivering 20 g. If you buy Sprout Living and want to keep exposure down, Original or Vanilla Lucuma are the sensible choices.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sprout Living's site also links a California Prop 65 notice. The product pages we read did not show which products it covers or the warning text, so we cannot say whether it applies to Epic Protein. Prop 65 thresholds are very strict, and many protein brands carry the warning; it is a prompt to ask for data rather than a sign of danger on its own.
          </p>

          <p>
            For readers who want published numbers, our guide to <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> lists brands that post lot results and explains how the Clean Label Project and Consumer Reports tested protein powders.
          </p>
        </div>

