        <div id="healthy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Is Sprout Living Epic Protein Healthy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, for most healthy adults Epic Protein is a healthy way to add plant protein, with a few numbers worth knowing. It is organic, low in sugar, free of gums and sugar alcohols, and provides fiber and iron. The flavored bags add 4 g of sugar from coconut sugar, which is about one teaspoon, and every flavor carries 310 to 380 mg of sodium per serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sodium is the figure people miss. At up to 380 mg per serving, one shake gives about 16 percent of the daily value, mostly from the pea protein and, in flavored bags, added Himalayan salt. That is not unusual for pea-based powders, but it matters if you are watching blood pressure or drink two shakes a day.
          </p>

          <p>
            The botanicals deserve some caution. Real Sport contains ashwagandha, which is not recommended in pregnancy and can interact with thyroid and sedative medicines, plus reishi and chaga mushrooms. The other superfoods, such as lucuma, baobab and acerola, are foods in small amounts rather than therapeutic doses. Anyone pregnant, with kidney disease, or on regular medication should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a daily protein shake, especially one with herbs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Allergy-wise, the bags say they are naturally free of gluten and other allergens but not certified, and that they are produced in a facility that may also process dairy, soy, wheat, tree nuts and peanuts. People with severe allergies should weigh that statement. For certified options, see our <a href="/best-organic-protein-powder/">best organic protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buyer-reviews" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Do Buyers Say About Sprout Living's Taste and Texture?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyer reviews of Epic Protein are strongly positive, with most praise for the clean ingredients and the smooth texture for a plant protein. Sprout Living's own site showed an average of about 4.9 out of 5 from more than 4,000 reviews when we checked, and retailer listings at Walmart showed similar averages for Chocolate Maca and Complete Coffee, though from far fewer reviews. Brand-site ratings are self-hosted, so treat them as a rough signal.
          </p>

          <p>
            The common praise is for taste without an artificial aftertaste, a creamy texture in smoothies, and the fact that Original blends into food without changing the flavor much. Chocolate Maca and Vanilla Lucuma come up most often as the favorite flavors in buyer reviews, with several reviewers saying Chocolate Maca is less gritty than other plant proteins they have tried.
          </p>

          <p>
            The complaints are mostly about price and earthiness. Some buyers find Original too plain or earthy to drink with water alone, and at least one review we found compared the texture to wet drywall with an earthy aftertaste, though it was not clear which flavor or formula it referred to. Others note that 20 g of protein in a 38 g scoop is low for the money. A reviewer at BarBend criticized the short list of micronutrients on the panel.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical reading: if you are new to plant protein, start with Vanilla Lucuma or Chocolate Maca in a smoothie with fruit and milk, not water. If you already like unflavored pea blends, Original will taste familiar and gives the most protein for the money.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-rivals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Sprout Living vs Garden of Life, Orgain and Sunwarrior
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sprout Living has the cleanest label of the four, Garden of Life Sport has the best testing, and Orgain and Sunwarrior are cheaper per gram. Which matters most decides which one to buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Garden of Life Sport Organic Plant-Based Protein gives about 30 g of protein per serving, is USDA Organic and is NSF Certified for Sport. It uses stevia and a longer ingredient list than Epic Original. If you train in a tested sport, it is the clear pick over Sprout Living. Orgain Organic Protein is the budget organic choice, at around $1.40 to $2.00 per 25 g per our <a href="/orgain-protein-powder-review/">Orgain review</a>, but many flavors use stevia with erythritol and an acacia-based creamer, the kind of additives Sprout Living avoids.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sunwarrior Warrior Blend, a pea and hemp protein sweetened with stevia, sits between them on price at roughly $2.00 to $2.50 per 25 g, per our <a href="/sunwarrior-protein-powder-review/">Sunwarrior review</a>. Its flavored tubs are sweetened with stevia, per our review, and it gives less protein per scoop than Epic Original.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are drawn to Sprout Living for its single-ingredient Simple Protein rather than Epic, our <a href="/best-pumpkin-seed-protein-powder/">best pumpkin seed protein powder</a> guide compares it with cheaper unflavored pumpkin seed powders on protein per scoop and cost per 25 g.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Sprout Living, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy Sprout Living if the ingredient list is your first filter. Epic Original is organic, vegan, unsweetened and has only six ingredients, and it still gives 26 g of protein per serving. Vanilla Lucuma and Chocolate Maca are among the few flavored plant proteins with no gums, natural flavors or sugar alcohols. If you avoid stevia entirely, note that every flavored Epic bag contains it; only Original and the unflavored Simple Protein bags are stevia-free.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you are a drug-tested athlete, because nothing in the range carries a sport certification. Skip it if you want lab results in hand before you buy, because Sprout Living asks you to trust its testing rather than showing it. And skip the flavored bags if cost per gram drives your choice; at $3.64 per 25 g they cost roughly twice what mainstream organic pea blends do.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a ranked list of vegan proteins across brands, with testing and cost per 25 g side by side, start with our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>
