        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Organic Means Tested for Metals</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The USDA Organic seal on Epic Protein covers how crops are grown, not what they absorb from soil. Lead and cadmium turn up in organic and conventional plant proteins alike. Sprout Living says it spot-tests for heavy metals, but publishes no results, so the organic badge alone tells you nothing about contaminant levels in your bag.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Flavors by Scoop Size</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every Epic bag uses the same 38 g serving, but protein ranges from 20 g in Vanilla Lucuma and Chocolate Maca to 26 g in Original. Over a 12-serving bag that is about 72 g of protein difference for the same price. If protein is the point, compare grams per serving first, then flavor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Missing the Stevia in Flavored Bags</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sprout Living markets Epic as having no erythritol or sugar alcohols, which is true, but every flavored bag we checked contains organic stevia leaf. Buyers who avoid stevia because of its aftertaste should stick to Original or the unflavored Simple Protein bags, which are unsweetened, and add fruit or dates in the blender.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overlooking the Sodium</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Epic Protein carries 310 to 380 mg of sodium per serving, up to 16 percent of the daily value. That is normal for pea protein but easy to miss on a powder marketed as pure and clean. Two shakes a day add up to around 750 mg, which matters if you are managing blood pressure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking Real Sport Without Reading the Herbs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Real Sport includes ashwagandha, reishi, chaga, turmeric and black pepper alongside the protein. Ashwagandha is not advised in pregnancy and can interact with thyroid and sedative medication. If you only want protein for recovery, Original does the job without any botanicals, at a lower cost per 25 g.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

